// Renovate, for this repository.
//
// Everything general lives in Lautstark/.github, which is where the preset
// moved on 2026-08-28. This repository and vorlaut-editor each held a
// byte-identical copy of it, which is the drift the move removes. What is left
// here is the part that is true of this repository and of no other, and that
// is the whole point of the split.
{
  $schema: "https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json",

  // The .json5 is spelled out on purpose: the //path/to/file syntax implies
  // a .json extension, so omitting it looks for a file that is not there
  // and Renovate stops before it does anything. The shorter "owner/repo:name"
  // form implies it too.
  extends: ["github>Lautstark/.github//renovate-shared.json5"],

  packageRules: [
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The shared packages: Renovate does not touch them
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // @lautstark/design is pinned as a github: dependency by release tag, and
    // is not on npm:
    //
    //     "@lautstark/design": "github:Lautstark/design#v1.17.0"
    //
    // It was four - bildquelle, sicherung and stimmquelle answered questions
    // the editor asks and went with it (adr/0012). The rule stays written for
    // the whole namespace rather than for the one package left, because which
    // of them this repository depends on is the thing that changes.
    //
    // Renovate can be made to follow those, but not with the npm manager that
    // finds them: it needs git-refs or github-tags wired up by hand, with a
    // versioning scheme told to it, per package. docs/packages.md already
    // records that neither Renovate nor Dependabot bumps a github: dependency
    // reliably, tag or sha.
    //
    // It is disabled here rather than configured, for a reason that is not
    // about the plumbing: **a bump of these is a decision, not an update.**
    // A refresh that moves section 1 or 2 of stimmquelle's contract
    // re-renders every recording on every device in the field. Its CHANGELOG
    // says which edits a consumer has to make, and it means it. A pull
    // request that arrives on a Monday morning and merges itself is the wrong
    // shape for that.
    //
    // What keeps the pins from going stale is a check rather than a service:
    //
    //     node node_modules/@lautstark/design/pins.js
    //
    // runs early in both workflows, asks each repository for its newest tag,
    // and names the pins that are behind. It warns and never fails.
    //
    // This rule is also the clearest thing that must NOT move into the shared
    // preset. It is about four specific packages and one specific contract,
    // and a repository that inherited it would be silently opting out of
    // updates to dependencies it does not have.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["@lautstark/**"],
      enabled: false,
    },

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // onnxruntime-web had a rule here, and no longer needs one
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // Its version was written in three places - package.json for the wasm
    // binaries piperVendor() copied into vendor/, src/backend/local.ts for the
    // CDN URL, and src/types/cdn.d.ts for that URL's module declaration - and
    // Renovate was disabled on it because bumping one of the three gives a
    // build where the wasm and the runtime are different releases of the same
    // library. Nothing fails at build time; the first sentence somebody tries
    // to speak does.
    //
    // All three of those are vorlaut-editor's now (adr/0012). This page speaks
    // nothing: it reads WAVs the editor already synthesised out of the package
    // and hands them to the device. The dependency is gone from package.json
    // with piperVendor(), so a rule naming it would match nothing - and a rule
    // that matches nothing is worse than none, because it reads as a hazard
    // somebody is still guarding against.
  ],
}
